[49m[38;5;10m+      1[0m:[38;5;10m[49m # Differential verification of the Rust translation of `c_src/src/luggage.c`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      2[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      3[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reference build: `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m → `c_src/build/driver`. `CMakeLists.txt` sets no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reference is compiled at **`-O0`** (gcc 11.5.0, glibc 2.34).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust build: `cd translation && cargo build --release` → `translation/target/release/driver`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both are driven as subprocesses by `translation/tests/differential.rs`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (36 tests, ~1900 input/argv combinations, no `#[ignore]`), which compares
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout, stderr and the exit status byte for byte. In addition ~65 000 randomised
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m inputs were run through an out-of-tree fuzzer during development with zero
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mismatches.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Uninitialised stack buffers observable on the first loop iteration
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom** — for stdin `abc\n` and argv `- - - -`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C    : "0000000000 \x03    abc\n"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust : "0000000000     abc\n"          <-- luggage id printed as empty
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause** — `main`'s per-iteration buffers are declared without an initialiser:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unsigned int time_stamp;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m char luggage_id[LUGGAGE_ID_LENGTH + 1];
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ...
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m comments[0] = 0;                      /* only `comments` is initialised */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A `scanf` *matching* failure (as opposed to an input failure) returns 0, not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `EOF`, so the C code does **not** `break`; it goes on to `strcpy` the buffer that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the failed conversion never touched. On the second and later iterations that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m buffer holds the previous record's value, which the translation already modelled
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m by hoisting the variables out of the loop. On the **first** iteration it holds
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m whatever main's stack frame contained, which the C standard leaves undefined.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The reference `-O0` build reproducibly leaves `time_stamp == 0`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `luggage_id == "\x03"` and `flight_id` / `departure` / `arrival` empty — leftovers
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m from libc start-up code that ran in the same stack region. Verified stable over
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 200 runs and unaffected by argv length, an empty environment (`env -i`) and a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3 KB environment variable.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix** — `src/main.rs` seeds `luggage_id` from `UNINIT_LUGGAGE_ID` (`b"\x03"`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and leaves the other buffers empty, so the reference build's observable output is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reproduced.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Caveat (this is genuinely undefined behaviour).** The byte is an artefact of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the reference build, not of the C source. Recompiling the same file at other
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m optimisation levels yields different garbage:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | build | first byte(s) of `luggage_id` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |-------|-------------------------------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-O0` (reference) | `03` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-O1` | `7c f3 b5 …` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-O2` | `cc 5b a3 …` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-O3` | `fc cc 60 …` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-Os` | `61 …` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Affected input classes: any stdin whose **first** record makes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf("%8[A-Z0-9] …")` fail to match — e.g. stdin starting with a lowercase
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m word, punctuation, or a bare `+`/`-` sign. Tested by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `matching_failure_on_first_iteration_uses_uninitialised_buffers`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. `strcpy` truncates the comment at an embedded NUL, `%80[^\n]` does not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom** — for stdin `5 LUG1 FL1 JFK LAX ab\0cd\n`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C    : "0000000005 LUG1 FL1 JFK LAX  ab\n"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust : "0000000005 LUG1 FL1 JFK LAX  ab\x00cd\n"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause** — the scanset `[^\n]` matches every byte except `\n`, including `\0`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so `scanf` stores the NUL in the local buffer and counts it against the field
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m width of 80. The subsequent `strcpy(new_directive->comments, comments)` then
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m copies only up to the first NUL, and `printf("%s")` / `strcmp` see the truncated
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m string. The translation was storing the full byte run.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix** — a `strcpy_bytes()` helper truncates at the first NUL, applied to all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m five fields when the directive is built. Truncation happens at *store* time, not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m at *scan* time, because the bytes after the NUL still consume field width and so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m still determine where the next conversion starts reading. Tested by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `nul_bytes_in_the_comment_truncate_the_stored_string`, including a case where the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m NUL is followed by 100 more bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. Exit status 0 instead of death by `SIGPIPE`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom** — with a large input and a reader that goes away
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`driver - - - - < big | head -c 20`):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C    : killed by SIGPIPE, shell reports status 141
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust : status 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause** — the Rust runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m writes to a broken pipe return `EPIPE`, which the program ignored before calling
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `exit(0)`. The C program inherits the default disposition and is killed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix** — `restore_default_sigpipe()` resets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_DFL` at the top of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` via a direct `signal(2)` call. Tested by `dying_on_a_closed_stdout_pipe`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note that a *closed* stdout (fd 1 not open at all) yields `EBADF` rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SIGPIPE`, and both programs exit 0 there — also checked.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours confirmed identical (no change needed)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These were the risky spots; each is covered by a named test.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`%d` into an `unsigned int *`.** glibc parses with `strtol` semantics and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   stores the result as an `int`, so out-of-range input clamps to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `LONG_MIN`/`LONG_MAX` and is then truncated: `99999999999999999999` →
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `4294967295`, `4294967296` → `0`, `-5` → `4294967291`. Decimal only, so `010`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is ten. A 10 000-digit number is handled without panicking (the accumulator
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   latches an overflow flag long before it could wrap).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`EOF` vs. matching failure.** Only `== EOF` breaks the read loop. All four
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `scanf` calls have their own EOF exit, and a partially assigned call still
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   returns ≥ 1 and therefore does *not* break — e.g. `55 LUG1` (flight id hits
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   EOF) falls through to the next `scanf`, which is the one that breaks.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **The dropped last record.** A final line without a trailing newline *and*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   without a comment makes `%80[^\n]` an input failure, so the otherwise complete
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   record is discarded. Adding a comment makes it a successful match and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   record survives.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **The double space before comments.** `%3[A-Z]` for the arrival is not followed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   by a whitespace directive, so `%80[^\n]` keeps the separating blank; the format
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   string then adds its own, producing `… LAX  comment`. With no comment at all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the line ends in a single trailing space.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Field widths and spill-over.** Conversions stop at 8/6/3/3/80 characters and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the leftovers are re-parsed by the *following* conversion, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `5 123456789 FL1 JFK LAX c` yields luggage id `12345678`, flight id `9`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   departure `FL`, arrival `1`, comment ` JFK LAX c`. A comment longer than 80
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   bytes leaves the tail in the stream, where it becomes the next record's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   timestamp.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`scanf` reads across newlines.** One field per line, blank lines between
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   fields, and several records on one line all parse the same way.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Whitespace class.** `\t`, `\n`, `\v`, `\f`, `\r` and space are all skipped by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the whitespace directives and by `%d`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`supersedes()` stops at the first later directive with a matching luggage id**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and reports "superseded" only if the departure also matches — even when a still
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   later directive would have matched. Preserved verbatim.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Insertion order.** `addRoutingDirectiveToList` inserts before the first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   strictly greater timestamp, so equal timestamps keep input order, and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   supersede scan walks the *sorted* list rather than the input order.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`matches()` wildcard.** Only a leading `-` is a wildcard (`-abc` is one too);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   an empty argument is not, and it matches a field that was never assigned.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **argv/stderr.** `argc != 5` writes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `Command line error: 4 arguments expected\n` to stderr and exits 1 without
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reading stdin. Checked for argc 1–4, 6 and 7. Non-UTF-8 argv bytes are compared
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   as raw bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`%010u` formatting**, including values above `INT_MAX` and exactly zero.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Known divergence that is not practically reachable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `addRoutingDirectiveToList` and `supersedes` are recursive with one frame per
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m list element (48 bytes per `supersedes` frame at `-O0`), so with the default
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 8 MiB stack the C program would overflow at roughly 175 000 directives, while the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust translation is iterative and would not.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m That threshold cannot be reached in practice: reaching a recursion depth of *n*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m also costs Θ(n²) work in `printMatchingDirectives`, so at n ≈ 175 000 the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program needs hours of CPU time before it could crash. Measured wall-clock:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m n = 20 000 completes in seconds and matches exactly; at n = 100 000 the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exceeds a 60 s budget while the Rust program finishes, which is a speed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m difference, not a behavioural one. The test suite therefore exercises volume at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m n = 400 (recursive insert at head, tail and middle, plus the O(n²) supersede
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m scan) and does not attempt to reproduce a stack overflow.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Reproducing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```sh
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .   # C reference
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd ../../translation && cargo build --release                           # Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cargo test --release                                                    # differential suite
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
